---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What TTL should load-balanced records use?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Low: 60-300 seconds, so failover and reweighting take effect quickly."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Can DNS failover replace a load balancer?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "No: DNS removes dead servers after detection delay, while a load balancer stops sending traffic to dead nodes per-connection, instantly."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Which providers support health checks?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Route 53, NS1, Constellix, and enterprise tiers of most managed DNS providers. Free tiers generally do not."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How do CDN and DNS balancing interact?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The CDN does per-request balancing at the edge; your DNS points users at the nearest edge. Both layers, different jobs."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is split-horizon DNS?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Different answers for internal vs external queries: internal users get private IPs, external users get public ones."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="DNS Load Balancing: Techniques Compared"
  description="DNS load balancing explained: round-robin, weighted, latency-based, and health-checked failover, with the limits of each approach."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ip-and-networking/" class="hover:text-accent">IP & Networking</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">DNS Load Balancing</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DNS Load Balancing</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">DNS can distribute traffic across servers by returning different answers to the same query. It is the simplest load balancing layer that exists, with sharp limits you must design around.</p>
    </div>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#the-techniques">The techniques</a></li>
        <li><a href="#the-limits-you-must-respect">The limits you must respect</a></li>
        <li><a href="#the-practical-stack">The practical stack</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="the-techniques">The techniques</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60"><table class="pdns-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Round-robin</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Multiple A records, returned in rotation</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Simple; no health checking</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Weighted</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Traffic split by ratio</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Controlled rollout, canary releases</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Latency-based</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Answer from the fastest region for each user</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Requires provider support (Route 53)</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Geo / geoproximity</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Answer based on user location</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Data residency, regional content</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Health-checked failover</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Monitor endpoints; remove dead ones automatically</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Real resilience (provider feature)</td></tr><tr><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Weighted + health</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Canary with automatic rollback</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Advanced (Route 53, NS1)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="the-limits-you-must-respect">The limits you must respect</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li class="leading-relaxed">Resolvers cache answers for the TTL: failover takes at least one TTL to propagate everywhere.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Round-robin keeps sending traffic to a dead server until you remove its record.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">DNS balancing balances QUERIES, not connections: a single user makes one lookup and hammers one server.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="the-practical-stack">The practical stack</h2>
      <p>For anything beyond hobby scale: DNS-level distribution (geo or weighted) pointing at health-checked load balancers, which handle per-connection decisions. DNS steers regions; load balancers steer sessions. Verify both layers with the <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a> after changes.</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What TTL should load-balanced records use?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Low: 60-300 seconds, so failover and reweighting take effect quickly.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can DNS failover replace a load balancer?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: DNS removes dead servers after detection delay, while a load balancer stops sending traffic to dead nodes per-connection, instantly.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Which providers support health checks?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Route 53, NS1, Constellix, and enterprise tiers of most managed DNS providers. Free tiers generally do not.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How do CDN and DNS balancing interact?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The CDN does per-request balancing at the edge; your DNS points users at the nearest edge. Both layers, different jobs.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is split-horizon DNS?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Different answers for internal vs external queries: internal users get private IPs, external users get public ones.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/dns-propagation-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Propagation checker →</a></li><li><a href="/dns-load-balancing/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DNS failover guide →</a></li><li><a href="/cloudflare-vs-route53/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Provider comparison →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
